.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
The singleton \fBErrorHandler\fP implements an issue reporting scheme to support platform-specific message capabilities like error windows or specific options that can be specified for core-dumps etc\&. The error handler further documents the progression of issues that occur during a run so that warnings etc\&. leading up to an error can be examined in the runtime\&.log file\&. Thus, the aim is to provide diagnostics of when and under what conditions an error arose\&. 


.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthai 

.PP
Stephen G\&. Roberts 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
1999
.RE
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The \fBErrorHandler\fP classifies (by enumeration), the different messages that are sent to it as INFOs, WARNINGs, ERRORs, and FATAL_ERRORs:

.PP
.PP
.nf
enum csmp_error{ INFO, EXCEPTION, WARNING, ERROR, FATAL_ERROR };
.fi
.PP

.PP
If a FATAL_ERROR is reported, the \fBErrorHandler\fP will terminate the run\&. This role is given to the error handler with its future role as exception handler in mind\&. Present FATAL_ERRORs will become recoverable once exception handling is consistently implemented throughout CSMP\&.
.SH "Design Intent"
.PP
The \fBErrorHandler\fP logs information strings to 'cout' / 'cerr' and to the log file 'ErrorHandler\&.log'\&. To be able to trace a message to its source, a string that consists of the class and method name from which the message originated must be supplied as first message argument\&. In case of an overloaded method additional information is included in braces which identifies the called method uniquely, for example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
ErrorHandler::Notice(char*, char*):
.fi
.PP

.PP
Messages which are written to file, are automatically preceded by a time string which tells the user when the message originated\&. Since messages may also contain numerical data in the description of the error message the \fBErrorHandler\fP interface is overloaded to accept std::string arguments (std::string has overloaded operators that allow the method writer to convert numbers to strings)\&. The functionality of the \fBErrorHandler\fP is planned to be extended to handle top level (C++) exceptions\&. For this purpose the standard exception base classes exception, logic_error, and runtime_error will be subclassed for CSMP-specific error types\&.

.PP
A goal of the exception handling is to store the transient state of a run before it gets aborted such that it can be carried on from this state at a later time, when the error condition has been properly diagnosed\&.
.SH "Applicability"
.PP
The \fBErrorHandler\fP is used inside high-level CSMP objects like the \fBModel\fP etc\&.\&. Only those which carry enough information so that it makes sense to collect user-readable diagnostics from a CSMP run are logged\&. Atomic generic classes report errors directly to the 'cerr' stream\&.
.SH "Structure"
.PP
\fBErrorHandler\fP is a base-level class using STL maps to store the different types of error messages\&.
.SH "Participants"
.PP
A singleton instance(static) is created upon loading the csmp core library\&.
.SH "Collaborations"
.PP
\fBErrorHandler\fP uses the functionality of STL maps and the std::string class\&. The \fBStandard_IO_Handler\fP is used in dialogs with a console user\&. The object \fBBE_Time\fP is contained to log the time at which errors occur\&.
.SH "Consequences"
.PP
The consequent usage of the \fBErrorHandler\fP turns it into an 'Observer' object the functionality of which may be widely extended in future implementations\&. For the time being, the user can use the '*\&.log' file and the screen output to obtain a detailed progress report for his/her CSMP run\&. Interaction with the run, however, is still very limited\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The \fBErrorHandler\fP stores the message strings in 3 maps corresponding to the message types: INFO, WARNING, ERROR\&. Since only a single FATAL_ERROR can occur before the run is terminated, it is lumped into the ERROR map\&. In the log file, all messages are logged sequentially\&.

.PP
The error log file is written by the destructor of the error handler\&. Because the destructor of a global object is called last, this assures that all messages that arise in the destruction process are logged as well\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The \fBErrorHandler\fP is implemented as a Singleton pattern (GoF)\&.

.PP
A reference to the ErrorHanlder instance is acquired as such

.PP
.PP
.nf
#include "ErrorHandler\&.h"
\&.\&.\&.
ErrorHandler& csmp_error( ErrorHandler::Instance() );
\&.\&.\&.
csmp_error\&.Note( ERROR, "MyClass::ProblematicMethod()", "what actually went wrong" );
.fi
.PP

.PP
after which one can call the handler

.PP
.PP
.nf
csmp_error\&.Note( ERROR, "IterativeAlgorithm<dim>::Iterations", "Solution did not converge! " );
.fi
.PP

.PP
alternatively, if i\&.e\&. just a single call is to be made

.PP
.PP
.nf
ErrorHandler::Instance()\&.Note( ERROR, "IterativeAlgorithm<dim>::Iterations", "Solution did not converge! " );
.fi
.PP

.PP
