.PP
 
.SS "void csmp::ErrorHandler::Out () const"

.PP
writes contents of contained maps to file The standard CSmP interface used to list the state of the object\&. Here, all recorded messages are printed on 'stdout' in chronological sequence\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ErrorHandler::PrintInfos () const"
To print messages of a specific type, such as INFO, as a list ordered by the object and method names from which the messages originated\&. This is useful, for instance, to test whether a new object performed in the expected way during a complex simulation\&.

.PP
Apart from \fBPrintInfos()\fP there are methods which print warnings (PrintWarnings()), and for errors\&. 
.SS "\fBCSMP_OUTPUT_LEVEL\fP csmp::ErrorHandler::ReportingLevel () const"

.PP
returns the current setting of the \fBCSMP_OUTPUT_LEVEL\fP Returns the current level of error reporting 
.SS "void csmp::ErrorHandler::ReportingLevel (\fBCSMP_OUTPUT_LEVEL\fP verb)"

.PP
sets output to corresponding \fBCSMP_OUTPUT_LEVEL\fP Sets the level of error reporting 
.SS "void csmp::ErrorHandler::Verbose (bool verb)"

.PP
sets output reporting level to verbose (reporting of all events to cout and cerr) Method to suppress runtime error messages\&. The latter can still be output selectively by listing the message maps using the Print\&.\&.\&.() methods\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ErrorHandler::WriteErrorsToFile (const char * err_file = \fR'ErrorHandler\&.log'\fP)"

.PP
writes the content of message containers stored by the handler to file OS-specific program termination prompted by an error classification as FATAL_ERROR\&. Before program terminates, this method writes all the messages which occurred until then to the text file 'ErrorHandler\&.log'\&. This is similar to calling the destructor of the error handler\&. 
.br
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Terminate() will report if it cannot open the output file and prompt the user for a new file name such that the event sequence until the error occurred can still be recorded\&. 

